---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Masthead and editorial principles of The Cochinchine Pensée."
>
  <article class="about">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="about__header">
        <p class="about__eyebrow">Masthead</p>
        <h1 class="about__title">Editorial Principles</h1>
      </header>

      <div class="about__body prose">
        <p class="lead">
          The Cochinchine Pensée is a Vietnamese-language essay publication in the
          tradition of Claremont Review of Books, The American Scholar, and the
          Times Literary Supplement. It exists for one purpose: to publish long-form
          essays of intellectual seriousness about politics, literature, and the
          Vietnamese intellectual tradition.
        </p>

        <h2>What we publish</h2>
        <p>
          Essays between 3,000 and 7,000 words. We do not publish news, reviews of
          contemporary events for their own sake, or work that mistakes polemic
          for argument. Each essay is held to a falsifiable thesis — a verdict
          the author is willing to defend — and the body of the essay is the
          defense.
        </p>

        <p>
          Vietnamese evidence carries equal weight to the Western canon. The
          sequence matters: in modern Vietnam, the state demolished civil
          society first; the market only colonized the vacuum. We do not invert
          this.
        </p>

        <h2>What we will not publish</h2>
        <p>
          Decorative rhetoric that earns its place only by sounding learned.
          Meta-commentary on the essay's own machinery. The short sentence
          manufactured into a punchline. We are a seasoned critic, not an
          amateur displaying range.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial cadence</h2>
        <p>
          One or two essays per week, published without a fixed schedule. Each
          essay goes through an editorial pipeline: brief, research dossier,
          architecture (movement model from a library of thirty-three named
          architectures), segmented drafting, assembly, and three parallel
          audits (argument, voice, fact) before publication.
        </p>

        <h2>Language</h2>
        <p>
          Essays are published in Vietnamese. Site chrome — navigation, footer,
          buttons, metadata — is in English, in keeping with the publication's
          international academic orientation. The Vietnamese intellectual
          tradition has always been in conversation with the world; the
          interface reflects that.
        </p>

        <h2>Editor</h2>
        <p>
          The publication is edited and largely written by its sole proprietor,
          M. Q. Doan. The masthead does not list a board of advisors, a
          distinguished committee, or a fellowship. There is one author. The
          standards are not lowered on that account.
        </p>

        <h2>How to read</h2>
        <p>
          Begin anywhere. The essays are not arranged in series and do not
          require prior reading. Each stands alone and presents its own
          argument in full. The reader-customizer at the bottom of each essay
          lets you adjust font size, measure, and theme — preferences persist
          between visits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: var(--sp-12) var(--sp-16);
  }

  .about__header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-10);
    padding-bottom: var(--sp-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-hair);
  }

  .about__eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-kicker);
    font-weight: var(--w-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tr-kicker);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-crimson);
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-3);
  }

  .about__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 2vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ink-primary);
  }

  .about__body {
    /* inherits .prose from prose.css */
  }
</style>
